Cisco's advisory of 2026-06-17 covers two flaws in Identity Services Engine (ISE) and ISE Passive Identity Connector, and says they are not dependent on one another: exploiting one is not required to exploit the other ([Cisco, 2026-06-17](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-ise-multi-G5WP8vv)). CVE-2026-20181 (CVSS 9.1) lets a remote attacker who holds valid administrative credentials run commands on the underlying operating system and elevate to root through a crafted HTTP request, because of insufficient input validation; in a single-node deployment it can also take the node down ([Cisco, 2026-06-17](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-ise-multi-G5WP8vv)). CVE-2026-20190 (CVSS 7.5) lets an unauthenticated remote attacker read sensitive information, including hashed credentials, through an authorization flaw in ISE 3.4 and 3.5; 3.3 and earlier are not vulnerable ([Cisco, 2026-06-17](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-ise-multi-G5WP8vv)). The first fixed releases are 3.1 Patch 12, 3.2 Patch 11, 3.3 Patch 11, 3.4 Patch 6 and 3.5 Patch 4 for CVE-2026-20181, and 3.4 Patch 6 and 3.5 Patch 3 for CVE-2026-20190; there is no workaround, and Cisco is not aware of malicious use ([Cisco, 2026-06-17](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-ise-multi-G5WP8vv)). A hot patch for 3.5 was available before Patch 4 ([SecurityWeek, 2026-06-18](https://www.securityweek.com/critical-command-execution-vulnerability-patched-in-cisco-ise/)).

**Exposure:** every ISE and ISE-PIC deployment on 3.1 to 3.5 below the fixed patch for CVE-2026-20181, which needs administrative credentials; ISE 3.4 and 3.5 below the fixed patch for CVE-2026-20190, which needs none ([Cisco, 2026-06-17](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-ise-multi-G5WP8vv)).

**Detection:** the ISE administration interface's web access and administrative audit logs are where both flaws surface: crafted requests to the administrative HTTP interface for the command-execution flaw, and unauthenticated requests that return data for the disclosure flaw.

**Defender takeaway:** confirm every ISE node runs at least the fixed patch for its train; on 3.4 and 3.5 nodes that ran an unfixed build with an internet- or user-network-reachable interface, treat the hashed credentials the disclosure flaw exposes as candidates for rotation.
